{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And all his daughters.</b> Rabbi Yehudah says: twin sisters were born with every tribe, and they married them. Rabbi Nechemiah says: they were Canaanite women. Rather, what is וְכָל בְּנֹתָיו? — his daughters-in-law, for a person does not refrain from calling his son-in-law his son and his daughter-in-law his daughter." "<b>And he refused to be comforted.</b> A person does not accept consolation for one who is alive but presumed dead — for concerning the dead a decree was decreed that he be forgotten from the heart, but not concerning the living (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>I will go down to my son.</b> Like עַל בְּנִי, 'concerning my son.' And there are many instances where אֶל serves in the sense of עַל: ״אֶל שָׁאוּל וְאֶל בֵּית הַדָּמִים״ (II Samuel 21), ״אֶל הִלָּקַח אֲרוֹן הָאֱלֹהִים וְאֶל מוֹת חָמִיהָ וְאִישָׁהָ״ (I Samuel 4)." "<b>Surely I will go down.</b> At its plain sense, it is an expression of burial — בְּאֶבְלִי אֶקָּבֵר, 'in my mourning I will be buried,' and I will not be comforted all my days. And its midrash: Gehinnom. This sign was given into my hand from the mouth of the Almighty — if not one of my sons dies in my lifetime, I am assured that I will not see Gehinnom." "<b>And his father wept for him.</b> Yitzchak — he was weeping on account of the trouble of Jacob his son, but he did not mourn, for he knew that he was alive."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:36.726189755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}